<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The whole factory on one timer: every gaffer whose config names this machine
  as home, followed by its floor watcher. `./factory` is idempotent, so this
  starts what is down and leaves what is up alone.

  300s is the resolution, and at this cadence it is close to the pace as well.
  What the operator feels is how long an approved plan sits before anything
  happens, and every beat that runs is a model invocation — so five minutes is
  a trade, not a default nobody thought about. Each iteration still decides
  whether its fire does any work: under the one-shot runtime it returns the
  interval it wants next, and fires that arrive before that has elapsed exit
  immediately. For resident-runtime gaffers this stays what it always was: a
  ceiling on how long a dead loop stays dead.

  --no-picker is what makes this the launchd form: start everything, attach to
  nothing. Fix the paths and:  launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.hev.factory.plist

  One timer covers the whole machine. If you would rather have one per gaffer,
  copy this to com.hev.factory.<name>.plist and give each a different Label —
  but `./factory` already loops over every config, so this is less to maintain.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.hev.factory</string>
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/bin/bash</string>
		<string>/Users/you/workspace/factory/factory</string>
		<string>--no-picker</string>
	</array>
	<key>StartInterval</key>
	<integer>300</integer>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>
	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>/tmp/factory-up.log</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>/tmp/factory-up.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
